---
// Local AI for Type Design with the Runebender Font Editor. Workshop
// slides for ATypI 2026 Sharjah. About 25 minutes of slides: where models are stored, the
// hardware they run on, the training loop, and the two ways to connect
// a model to the editor.
// Companion to runebender.org/docs/local-ai-setup.html and the demo repo.
import Fig from '@/components/slides/Fig.astro'
import Slide from '@/components/slides/Slide.astro'
import SlidesLayout from '@/layouts/SlidesLayout.astro'

import endRunebender from '@/assets/slides/localai/end-runebender.jpg'
import grid from '@/assets/slides/localai/runebender-xilem-grid.png'
import nodes from '@/assets/slides/localai/runebender-xilem-nodes.png'
import nufoEditing from '@/assets/slides/localai/runebender-xilem-nufo-editing.png'
import outline from '@/assets/slides/localai/runebender-xilem-outline.png'
import titleRunebender from '@/assets/slides/localai/title-runebender.jpg'
---

<SlidesLayout
  title="Local AI for Type Design with the Runebender Font Editor (slides)"
  description="Workshop slides for Local AI for Type Design with the Runebender Font Editor, ATypI 2026 Sharjah."
  running="Local AI for Type Design"
  author="Eli Heuer"
>
  <!-- ── Opening ── -->

  <Slide label="Title">
    <img
      class="bleed"
      src={titleRunebender.src}
      alt="Runebender with a bismillah labeled for training: each letter’s ink inside a colored polygon."
    />
    <div class="c-1-9 over-bleed">
      <h1 class="t-display descends">Local AI for<br />Type Design</h1>
      <p class="t-head">with the Runebender Font Editor</p>
    </div>
    <div class="c-1-6 over-bleed at-bottom">
      <p class="t-body">Eli Heuer · ATypI 2026 Sharjah</p>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Runebender is a font editor written in Rust. This workshop covers small models that run on your own computer, trained on your own drawings, and used from inside the editor.</p>
      <p>Setup instructions are at runebender.org/docs/local-ai-setup.html. The examples are in the runebender-local-ai-demo repository.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Overview" label="Overview">
    <div class="c-1-10 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">Overview</h2>
      <p class="t-body">
        In Runebender, a model is a folder of files on your disk. The editor
        reads it from a fixed location. No account or network connection is
        needed.
      </p>
      <p class="t-body">
        This workshop covers why local models are useful for type design, what
        hardware runs them, how to set them up, four examples in the editor,
        and how external agents connect to the editor.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>A Runebender model folder holds three or four files: a config, the weights, a vocabulary, and sometimes a manifest.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ── Why local ── -->

  <Slide section="Why local" label="Why local">
    <div class="c-1-4 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">Why local</h2>
      <p class="t-small">
        The training data is the designer’s own drawings. A model trained on
        one font learns one set of drawing conventions.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        The font models are small: one to a few dozen megabytes. They train in
        minutes on a laptop GPU.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        The editor downloads nothing and sends nothing. Model folders can be
        opened and inspected like any other folder.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Fig
      class="c-5-12 fig-right"
      src={nufoEditing}
      alt="Runebender editing Arabic glyphs with a rendered preview."
    />
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Most font generation research trains on large collections of scraped fonts, which mix many drawing conventions. These models train on original drawings made on one grid.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Why local" label="The training loop">
    <div class="c-1-4 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">The training loop</h2>
      <p class="t-small">
        The designer draws a core set of glyphs. The editor drafts the rest.
        The designer reviews and corrects the drafts. Each accepted draft is
        added to the training data.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        The designer reviews shapes. Recurring errors get a detector or a
        repair tool before the next training run.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="c-5-12">
      <table class="mono-table">
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>1</td><td>Draw and grade</td><td>A reference set in one master, marked green</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2</td><td>Train</td><td>The model trains on the green set only</td></tr>
          <tr><td>3</td><td>Draft</td><td>10 to 20 glyphs on the 8-unit grid, as a proposal</td></tr>
          <tr><td>4</td><td>Review and fix</td><td>Correct, install, mark orange</td></tr>
          <tr><td>5</td><td>Grade</td><td>Corrected drafts are marked green and join the set</td></tr>
          <tr><td>6</td><td>Build</td><td>Compile the font and proof it in text</td></tr>
          <tr><td>7</td><td>Diagnose</td><td>Name each recurring error and add a check for it</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Step 5 feeds step 1. Each training run has more green glyphs than the last, so later drafts need fewer corrections.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Why local" label="Rules for machine output">
    <div class="c-1-10 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">Rules for machine output</h2>
      <p class="t-body">
        A draft is a proposal, not a direct edit. Install and Undo use the
        ordinary proposal flow.
      </p>
      <p class="t-body">
        Only a person marks a glyph green. Machine drafts stay orange until
        they are graded.
      </p>
      <p class="t-body">
        Drafts land on the 8-unit grid. The designer makes the 2-unit optical
        corrections.
      </p>
      <p class="t-body">
        Each engine is scored against drawn pairs. The baseline is a plain
        mean shift.
      </p>
      <p class="t-body">Nothing writes font files. The designer saves.</p>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>The same rules apply to the local models, to agents over MCP, and to the Chat panel.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ── Hardware ── -->

  <Slide section="Hardware" label="Memory size and bandwidth">
    <div class="c-1-4 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">Memory size and bandwidth</h2>
      <p class="t-small">
        Memory size sets which models fit. At 4 bits, a model takes about
        0.6 GB per billion parameters, plus space for the context.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        Memory bandwidth sets the speed. Each new token reads the active
        weights once, so tokens per second is at most bandwidth divided by
        their size.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="c-5-12">
      <table class="mono-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Model</th><th>Parameters</th><th>File</th><th>Kind</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>qwen3.5-4b</td><td>4B</td><td>2.9 GB</td><td>dense</td></tr>
          <tr><td>gpt-oss-20b</td><td>21B</td><td>12.1 GB</td><td>mixture of experts, 3.6B active</td></tr>
          <tr><td>gemma-4-26b-a4b</td><td>26B</td><td>17.0 GB</td><td>mixture of experts, 4B active</td></tr>
          <tr><td>qwen3.6-27b</td><td>27B</td><td>17.6 GB</td><td>dense</td></tr>
          <tr><td>qwen3.6-35b-a3b</td><td>35B</td><td>22.4 GB</td><td>mixture of experts, 3B active</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>For people new to local models: the processor matters less than the size and speed of the memory the model can use.</p>
      <p>These are the chat models in my ~/Runebender/models folder, as 4-bit GGUF files. The file size is the memory the weights need.</p>
      <p>The operating system and the context also need memory. On a 16 GB machine, keep the model under about 10 GB.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Hardware" label="Dense and mixture of experts">
    <div class="c-1-4 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">Dense and mixture of experts</h2>
      <p class="t-small">
        A dense model reads every weight for every token. A mixture of experts
        reads only a few experts per token.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        qwen3.6-35b-a3b has 35B parameters and reads about 3B per token. It
        needs as much memory as a large model, but generates tokens about as
        fast as a small one.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="c-5-12 stack">
      <table class="mono-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>MacBook Pro, M4 Pro, 273 GB/s</th><th>Read per token</th><th>Upper limit</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>qwen3.6-27b, dense</td><td>17.6 GB</td><td>about 15 tokens/s</td></tr>
          <tr><td>qwen3.6-35b-a3b, mixture of experts</td><td>about 2 GB</td><td>about 130 tokens/s</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <table class="mono-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Measured on AMD Strix Halo, 128 GB</th><th>Speed</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Qwen3 30B-A3B, mixture of experts</td><td>about 100 tokens/s</td></tr>
          <tr><td>gpt-oss-120b, mixture of experts</td><td>about 55 tokens/s</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Llama 3.3 70B, dense</td><td>about 5 tokens/s</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>The upper limit is bandwidth divided by the bytes read per token. Measured speed is lower. On the same machine, the mixture of experts is several times faster than the dense model.</p>
      <p>Many current open models are mixtures of experts, including gpt-oss and the Qwen3 A3B models. This is why machines with large unified memory are useful for local inference.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Hardware" label="Three kinds of machine">
    <div class="c-1-4 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">Graphics card</h2>
      <p class="t-small">
        24 to 32 GB of fast memory on one consumer card.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        NVIDIA’s CUDA is supported by all inference and training tools. The
        card goes in a PC that can be built and upgraded.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="c-5-8 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">Unified memory</h2>
      <p class="t-small">
        The processor and the graphics share one pool of 64 to 512 GB.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        Lower bandwidth than a graphics card, but larger models fit. Macs, AMD
        Strix Halo, NVIDIA DGX Spark.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="c-9-12 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">Multiple cards</h2>
      <p class="t-small">
        Several cards in one machine, or a workstation chip with datacenter
        memory.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        For labs and teams. tinybox, RTX Pro 6000 builds, DGX Station.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Inference works on all three. Training has the best support on NVIDIA. Small models, such as the Runebender font models, also train on Apple Metal.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Hardware" label="Desktop machines up to 128 GB">
    <div class="c-1-12 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">Desktop machines up to 128 GB</h2>
      <table class="mono-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Machine</th><th>Memory</th><th>Bandwidth</th><th>Price</th><th>Use</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>PC with a used RTX 3090</td><td>24 GB</td><td>936 GB/s</td><td>$700 to $1,050 per card</td><td>Lowest cost per GB, CUDA</td></tr>
          <tr><td>PC with an RTX 5090</td><td>32 GB</td><td>1,792 GB/s</td><td>$2,400 to $4,300 per card</td><td>Fastest consumer card</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Strix Halo mini PC</td><td>128 GB</td><td>256 GB/s</td><td>from about $2,000</td><td>120B mixtures of experts</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Framework Desktop, Max+ 395</td><td>128 GB</td><td>256 GB/s</td><td>$3,449</td><td>Same chip, repairable</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Mac Studio, M5 Max</td><td>36 to 128 GB</td><td>460 to 614 GB/s</td><td>from $2,499</td><td>MLX and llama.cpp, low noise</td></tr>
          <tr><td>NVIDIA DGX Spark</td><td>64 or 128 GB</td><td>273 GB/s</td><td>$3,999 at launch, now higher</td><td>CUDA, two units link</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="t-credit">
        US prices, October 2026, from vendor pages and retail listings. Memory
        shortages raised many of these prices during 2026.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Strix Halo is the AMD Ryzen AI Max+ 395. GMKtec, HP, Beelink, Minisforum and Framework sell machines with the same chip.</p>
      <p>The DGX Spark is the only machine in this table with both CUDA and 128 GB. Its bandwidth is close to Strix Halo’s. The extra cost buys the NVIDIA software stack and the option to link two units for 256 GB.</p>
      <p>A 64 GB DGX Spark from OEM partners started at $4,999 in October 2026.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Hardware" label="Larger machines">
    <div class="c-1-12 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">Larger machines</h2>
      <table class="mono-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Machine</th><th>Memory</th><th>Bandwidth</th><th>Price</th><th>Use</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Framework Desktop, PRO 495</td><td>192 GB</td><td>about 273 GB/s</td><td>$6,799 kit</td><td>Ships November 2026</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Mac Studio, M5 Ultra</td><td>96 to 512 GB</td><td>1.2 TB/s</td><td>from $5,499</td><td>Largest models in one machine</td></tr>
          <tr><td>PC with RTX Pro 6000 Blackwell</td><td>96 GB per card</td><td>1,792 GB/s</td><td>$9,000 to $16,000 per card</td><td>70B at high precision, one card</td></tr>
          <tr><td>tinybox green v2</td><td>4 × RTX 5090, 128 GB</td><td>1,792 GB/s per card</td><td>about $45,000</td><td>Prebuilt multi-card machine</td></tr>
          <tr><td>NVIDIA DGX Station, GB300</td><td>748 GB, 252 GB of it HBM</td><td>7.1 TB/s</td><td>$85,000 to $160,000</td><td>Datacenter GPU in a workstation</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Mac Pro</td><td></td><td></td><td>Discontinued March 2026</td><td>Replaced by the Mac Studio</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="t-credit">
        US prices, October 2026. The 512 GB M5 Ultra is expected above $10,000.
        DGX Station prices are set by OEM partners.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>The M5 Ultra has 1.2 TB/s at every memory size. More memory adds capacity, not speed.</p>
      <p>tinybox is made by tiny corp, the company behind tinygrad. Their red models use AMD cards and cost less.</p>
      <p>The DGX Station bandwidth figure is for its 252 GB of HBM. The other 496 GB is LPDDR5X at 396 GB/s.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Hardware" label="A Linux PC">
    <div class="c-1-6 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">A Linux PC</h2>
      <p class="t-small">
        A common starting point is one used RTX 3090: 24 GB at 936 GB/s for
        about $1,000. A second card brings the total to 48 GB. llama.cpp
        splits a model across cards.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        The graphics card does the work. The other parts supply power, space,
        and data.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        Most training tools are developed and tested on Linux with CUDA first.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="c-7-12">
      <table class="mono-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Part</th><th>Requirement</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Graphics card</td><td>Memory first, then bandwidth</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Power supply</td><td>1000 W or more for two cards</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Motherboard</td><td>Space for two wide cards</td></tr>
          <tr><td>System memory</td><td>64 GB or more</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Disk</td><td>2 TB NVMe for model files</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Processor</td><td>Any recent desktop processor</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>An RTX 3090 draws about 350 W. Two cards need a large power supply and good airflow.</p>
      <p>AMD cards work with llama.cpp through Vulkan and ROCm. Training tools support them less well.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Hardware" label="My setup">
    <div class="c-1-4 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">My setup</h2>
      <p class="t-small">
        The Runebender font models are one to a few dozen megabytes. A
        consumer graphics card is enough to train them.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="c-5-12">
      <table class="mono-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Machine</th><th>Memory</th><th>Use</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>MacBook Pro, M4 Pro</td><td>48 GB, 273 GB/s</td><td>Chat and coding, qwen3.6-35b-a3b</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Linux PC, RTX 2060 SUPER</td><td>8 GB, 448 GB/s</td><td>Training, about 100 s per epoch</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Steam Deck</td><td>16 GB shared</td><td>qwen3.5-4b at 18 tokens/s</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>The Linux PC trained the Nasta’liq model in the Nasta’liq Distilled talk. The Steam Deck runs Runebender and a chat model with the Vulkan build of llama.cpp.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Hardware" label="Runtimes">
    <div class="c-1-4 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">Runtimes</h2>
      <p class="t-small">
        A runtime loads a model file and runs it. Most runtimes can also serve
        the model on a local address that other programs call.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        The Runebender Chat panel connects to llama.cpp this way.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="c-5-12">
      <table class="mono-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Runtime</th><th>Description</th><th>Hardware</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>llama.cpp</td><td>Runs GGUF files, the common format</td><td>Mac, NVIDIA, AMD, CPU</td></tr>
          <tr><td>MLX</td><td>Apple’s machine learning library</td><td>Mac</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Ollama, LM Studio</td><td>Desktop apps built on the two above</td><td>Mac, Windows, Linux</td></tr>
          <tr><td>vLLM</td><td>Serves many users at once</td><td>NVIDIA servers</td></tr>
          <tr><td>PyTorch, Candle</td><td>Training; font-ml uses Candle</td><td>NVIDIA, Mac</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Ollama and LM Studio are the simplest first step: install the app, choose a model, and start a chat.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Hardware" label="Choosing a machine">
    <div class="c-1-12 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">Choosing a machine</h2>
      <table class="mono-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Goal</th><th>Starting point</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>First experiments</td><td>The computer you have. A 4B model needs 3 GB.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Train small font models</td><td>An NVIDIA card with 8 GB or more, on Linux</td></tr>
          <tr><td>30B models at high speed</td><td>A 24 to 32 GB NVIDIA card, or a Mac with 48 GB or more</td></tr>
          <tr><td>120B models on a desktop</td><td>128 GB of unified memory: Strix Halo, Mac Studio, DGX Spark</td></tr>
          <tr><td>The largest open models</td><td>Mac Studio, M5 Ultra, 256 to 512 GB</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Teams, or training large models</td><td>RTX Pro 6000 builds, tinybox, DGX Station</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Start with the hardware you have. Buy new hardware when a real task is too slow, and choose by memory first.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ── Setup ── -->

  <Slide section="Setup" label="Installation">
    <div class="c-1-4 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">Installation</h2>
      <p class="t-small">
        <code>runebender</code> is the editor. <code>font-ml</code> runs and
        trains the models. Both build with stable Rust from rustup.rs.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        Supported hardware: an Apple Silicon Mac, a Linux PC with an NVIDIA
        card, or a CPU, which is slower.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="c-5-12 stack">
      <pre class="code">cargo install --git https://github.com/fontgarden/runebender
cargo install --git https://github.com/eliheuer/font-ml \
    --features metal     # or cuda, or no feature for CPU</pre>
      <pre class="code">runebender --help
font-ml tasks</pre>
      <pre class="code">git clone https://github.com/eliheuer/runebender-local-ai-demo
cd runebender-local-ai-demo
runebender fonts/Shapes.ufo</pre>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Use the metal feature on macOS, cuda on Linux with NVIDIA, and no feature for CPU.</p>
      <p>font-ml tasks lists the available tasks. Only tasks marked ready can be used.</p>
      <p>Compiling takes about fifteen minutes. For a workshop, install beforehand.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Setup" label="Model folders">
    <div class="c-1-4 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">Model folders</h2>
      <p class="t-small">
        Models are read from <code>~/Runebender/models/&lt;kind&gt;/</code>, or
        from <code>MyFont.models/</code> beside the font, so the model stays
        with the font.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        Each kind is a different type of model. A chat model cannot draw, and
        a shapes model cannot chat.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="c-5-12">
      <table class="mono-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Kind</th><th>Files</th><th>Source</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>shapes</td><td>config, weights, vocab, manifest</td><td>Local AI Shapes tool, from a UFO</td></tr>
          <tr><td>bolden</td><td>config, weights, vocab</td><td>font-ml run train, from two masters</td></tr>
          <tr><td>chat</td><td>model.gguf, tokenizer.json</td><td>Download; the demo script gets Qwen3</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>The editor reads only these files. The config is plain JSON.</p>
      <p>RUNEBENDER_MODELS sets a different model root for one run.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ── Examples ── -->

  <Slide section="Examples" label="Example 1: Local AI Shapes">
    <div class="c-1-4 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">Local AI Shapes</h2>
      <p class="t-small">
        Open a glyph, hold the shapes button, and choose Local AI Shapes.
        Without a model, the panel offers Train From the Sample Shapes.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        After training, choose a shape and drag a box. The model draws a
        preview in the box. Enter keeps it, Tab draws another, Esc removes it.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small muted">
        The model can draw only the shapes in the training font. To add a
        heart, draw one and click Add This Font’s Shapes.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Fig
      class="c-5-12 fig-right"
      src={outline}
      alt="Runebender editing an outline, with the right panel open."
    />
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>A quick training run takes about twelve minutes on a laptop GPU. A thorough run takes about thirty. get-base-model.sh in the demo repository downloads a trained base model with a checksum.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Examples" label="Example 2: Bolden">
    <div class="c-1-4 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">Bolden</h2>
      <p class="t-small">
        The model moves the points of a Regular glyph to make a Bold draft.
        The point structure stays the same, so the masters still interpolate.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        The Weight debt block lists Bold glyphs that are still identical to
        Regular. It drafts them in batches of 20 and marks them orange.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        About half a second per glyph on Metal.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="c-5-12 demo-side">
      <Fig
        class="fig-right"
        src={grid}
        alt="Runebender’s glyph overview grid for a Latin font."
      />
      <table class="mono-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Virtua Grotesk Bold</th><th>Owed</th><th>Drawn pairs</th><th>Offset vs model</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Latin</td><td>2</td><td>56</td><td>about even</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Arabic</td><td>11</td><td>130</td><td>offset is better</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Hebrew</td><td>71</td><td>5</td><td>offset is better</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Measured 2026-10-09 on Virtua Grotesk. Both engines are only slightly better than the mean shift. Hebrew has five drawn pairs. An engine needs 20 green pairs on a script, with 5 held out, before its drafts are used.</p>
      <p>The score is the distance between the draft and the outline the designer kept.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Examples" label="Example 3: chat model">
    <div class="c-1-4 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">Chat model</h2>
      <p class="t-small">
        A Qwen3 GGUF file from Hugging Face, in the same folder layout. The
        model answers questions about the open font with the same tools an
        agent uses.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        Small models cannot calculate a stem width from a point list. They do
        call the correct tools in the correct order. The tools must return the
        measurement directly.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="c-5-12">
      <table class="mono-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Engine</th><th>Model</th><th>Time</th><th>Stem of H</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>font-ml chat (Candle)</td><td>Qwen3 4B</td><td>543 s</td><td>74, wrong</td></tr>
          <tr><td>llama-server</td><td>Qwen3 4B</td><td>11 s</td><td>564, wrong: bounds width</td></tr>
          <tr><td>llama-server</td><td>Qwen3 8B</td><td>24 s</td><td>Subtracted sidebearings, wrong</td></tr>
          <tr><td>llama-server + analyze_glyph</td><td>Qwen3 4B</td><td>5 s</td><td>100, correct</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Same question, same copy of Virtua Grotesk, M4 Pro. Two decisions came from this test: use llama.cpp as the engine, and make the tools do the measuring.</p>
      <p>A 0.6B model gives wrong answers. The Chat panel was built and tested with 4B.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Examples" label="Example 4: node graphs">
    <div class="c-1-4 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">Node graphs</h2>
      <p class="t-small">
        The train and bolden steps as a graph in the Nodes tab. Change
        <code>steps</code> and run it again. The same graph file runs from the
        command line.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        A graph run does not save or change the open font. Results are
        candidates until they are installed.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Fig
      class="c-5-12 fig-right"
      src={nodes}
      alt="Runebender’s Nodes tab with a workflow graph."
    />
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>External tasks declare their inputs, so unchanged runs are cached. Model folders are hashed from their contents, not from the manifest.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ── Agents ── -->

  <Slide section="Agents" label="External agents over MCP">
    <div class="c-1-4 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">Agents over MCP</h2>
      <p class="t-small">
        One command registers the editor with Claude Code, Codex, Claude
        Desktop and Cursor. The agent works on the open document, including
        unsaved changes.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        45 tools: read, measure, proof, propose point edits, and fork
        experiments. Five workflows as prompts: review-glyph,
        compatibility-check, spacing-pass, kerning-pass, draft-weight.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="c-5-12 stack">
      <pre class="code">runebender mcp --install</pre>
      <table class="mono-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Kind of AI</th><th>Access to the font</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Local models</td><td>font-ml tasks in the editor: shapes, bolden, brush</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Agent in the repository</td><td>Edits GLIF files, plists, scripts, CI</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Agent in the editor</td><td>runebender mcp --live, proposals and receipts</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Vision model on proofs</td><td>Ranks drafts for review; does not grade</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Local chat</td><td>The Chat panel, without an external agent</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Figma and Penpot have MCP servers, Glyphs has a third-party one, and FontLab 9 has an in-app chat. Runebender adds a review step (proof, compare, grade) and a safety contract (proposals, receipts, no saves).</p>
      <p>The rules for machine output apply here too. The agent reads first and proofs the result. The designer installs.</p>
      <p>A model does not have to run inside Runebender. Any Python model can read the UFO and submit proposals through MCP.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ── Live ── -->

  <Slide section="Live" label="Runebender">
    <img
      class="bleed"
      src={endRunebender.src}
      alt="Runebender with a labeled bismillah and its preview."
    />
    <div class="c-1-9 over-bleed">
      <p class="t-display">Runebender</p>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Switch to Runebender. Open Shapes.ufo, choose Local AI Shapes, drag a box, type star. Then bolden octagon, undo, and bolden chevron.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="End" label="Links">
    <div class="c-1-10 stack-lg">
      <p class="t-display">Thank you</p>
      <p class="t-body">
        runebender.org/docs/local-ai-setup.html<br />
